insert into dp_voice_exercises(skill_id,title,instructions,minutes,is_active,visibility,curriculum_key)
select s.id,v.title,v.instructions,v.minutes,true,'assigned_only',v.key from (values
 ('breath-posture','stance-check','Balanced stance check','Stand with your feet about hip-width apart and your knees slightly soft. Let your arms hang. Imagine a string lifting the top of your head gently. Roll your shoulders back and down once, then let them rest. Check your jaw is loose. Say your name out loud, then sing one line of a song you know. Notice what stays relaxed.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',3),
 ('breath-posture','low-breath-release','Quiet low breath and steady ''ssss''','Place one hand lightly on your lower ribs and one on your stomach. Breathe in quietly through your nose or mouth for a count of four, letting the lower ribs and stomach widen a little. Do not lift your shoulders. Release the air on a soft, steady ''ssss'' for as long as is comfortable. Rest and repeat four times. Write down your longest comfortable release in seconds.

Care: Stop if you feel light-headed.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('breath-posture','count-and-hum','Counted breath into a hum','Breathe in for four counts, then hum one comfortable note on ''mm'' for eight counts with a gentle, steady sound. Feel a soft buzz in your lips and nose. Rest. Repeat on a slightly higher and then a slightly lower note. If humming feels tight, open your mouth a little as if saying ''ng'' in ''sing''.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('breath-posture','phrase-on-one-breath','Sing a phrase on one breath','Choose a four-bar phrase from a song you love. Mark where you will breathe with a small tick in the lyrics. Breathe in quietly, sing the phrase at a comfortable volume, and stop before you push. Record yourself. Listen: does the end of the phrase sound as relaxed as the beginning? Adjust your tick marks and try again.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('breath-posture','consonant-air','Spend the air wisely: consonants and vowels','Speak a line of a song with a flat, steady energy. Then sing it, keeping the same easy energy. Notice which words use up a lot of air (''h'', ''f'', ''s'', ''th''). Soften those consonants a little and let the vowels carry the sound. Mark the words where you tend to lose breath.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('breath-posture','breath-in-rhythm','Breathing in rhythm','Pick a faster song. Mark small, quick breaths in the gaps between lines. Practice breathing in time with the music: tap the beat, breathe in on the ''and'' of the beat before your entrance, and sing. Record a verse and listen for any rushed or noisy breaths.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('pitch-ear','match-one-note','Match one note','Play one note on a keyboard or tuning app that sits comfortably in your voice. Listen to it for two seconds. Imagine it in your head. Hum it softly. Hold your hum and listen to whether it blends with the note. If it sounds like a wobble or a clash, slide very slowly until it fits. Repeat with five different notes. Rest your voice if it feels tired.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('pitch-ear','higher-lower-same','Higher, lower or the same?','Ask a helper (or use a free ear-training app) to play two notes. Say whether the second is higher, lower or the same. Then sing both notes. Start with notes far apart and bring them closer together over the week. Keep a simple score each day.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('pitch-ear','echo-patterns','Echo patterns: 1-3-5-3-1','Listen to a short pattern played slowly (for example five notes up and down). Sing it back on ''ah'' or ''noo'' at a comfortable volume. Then try it a half-step higher and a half-step lower. Record one round and listen to whether each note was in tune.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('pitch-ear','a-cappella-line','A cappella first line','Choose the first line of a familiar song. Sing it without any accompaniment and record it. Then play the starting note and sing again with the track. Listen to both. Mark any word where your pitch moved. Practice those words slowly with a keyboard until they match.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('pitch-ear','interval-slides','Interval slides and landing','Play two notes a third apart (for example do and mi). Sing the first, then slide slowly to the second and stop exactly on it. Check the landing against the instrument. Repeat with fourths and fifths. Slow slides help your ear catch the exact note.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('pitch-ear','drone-harmony-pitch','Sing against a drone','Play one low note (a drone). Sing a slow scale above it and listen for how each note sounds against the drone, from easy and smooth to tense. Practice landing on the notes that feel smooth. Then sing a short melody you know over the drone.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('tone-resonance','yawn-sigh','Yawn-sigh release','Take an easy, quiet yawn (or imagine one). On the exhale let out a soft sigh on ''hah'' from a comfortable middle note down to a lower note. Keep your jaw loose and your tongue resting. Repeat four times and notice if your throat feels more open.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',3),
 ('tone-resonance','ng-glide','''ng'' glide','Say ''sing'' and hold the ''ng'' sound at the end (the back of the tongue touches the soft palate). Hum on ''ng'' on one comfortable note, then glide slowly up a few notes and back down. Feel the soft buzz behind your nose and mouth. Keep the sound gentle. Rest if it tickles.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',4),
 ('tone-resonance','hoo-sigh-glide','''Hoo'' sighs','Sing a soft ''hoo'' (like a gentle owl) from a comfortable note, gliding down slowly. Keep the sound round and quiet. Do five sighs on different starting notes. Focus on an easy release rather than power.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',4),
 ('tone-resonance','vowel-ladder','Vowel ladder','Sing one comfortable note on ''oo'', then ''oh'', ''ah'', ''eh'', ''ee'' without changing the sound too much. Keep the jaw relaxed and the tongue easy. Notice which vowel feels tighter. Record the five vowels and listen for differences in tone.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('tone-resonance','soft-to-medium','Soft, medium, steady','Sing a comfortable note on ''ah'' at a soft volume, then medium, then soft again, keeping the tone free and the same colour. Do not push. Record and listen: is the tone steady or does it get harsher at the medium level?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('tone-resonance','colour-choices','Choosing tone colours for a song','Pick one phrase of a song. Sing it three ways: bright and forward, warm and rounded, and simple and natural. Record all three. Choose the one that best fits the words and style and say why.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('range','range-map','Map your comfortable range','With a keyboard or app, start on a note in the middle of your speaking range. Sing it on ''ah''. Move one note down at a time until the sound gets uncomfortable, then one note back. Do the same going up. Write down your lowest and highest easy notes. Do not push beyond ease.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('range','gentle-sirens','Gentle sirens on ''ng'' or ''oo''','Sing a soft ''ng'' or ''oo'' and glide slowly up as far as is comfortable, then glide back down. Keep the sound soft and connected. Do four sirens. If it feels strained, make the glide smaller.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('range','five-note-steps','Five-note steps, one half-step at a time','Sing a five-note scale (do re mi fa sol and back) on ''noo'' or ''ah'' starting on a comfortable note. Move the whole pattern up by one note each time until it feels like effort, then back down. Stop one step before it feels hard. Notice how many steps you gained since last week.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('range','key-test','Find the best key for a song','Choose a song. Sing the chorus in three different keys (the original, one step lower, one step higher). Record each. Pick the key where you sound the clearest and most comfortable and write down the key.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('range','range-in-song','Use the new edge notes in a song','Pick a song that touches the edges of your range. Practice the phrases that reach the highest and lowest notes slowly on ''noo'', then on words. Make sure the tone stays steady. Record a verse and chorus and mark any place that sounds strained.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('registers','speak-to-sing','Speak to sing','Say a short line (like ''Hello, how are you today?'') in your normal voice. Say it again slightly higher and lighter, as if calling to someone across the room. Now sing the line on one note, then on a gentle rise. Notice what changes in your throat and chest. Keep everything easy.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',4),
 ('registers','gentle-glide-oo','Gentle glides on ''oo'' and ''ng''','Sing a soft ''oo'' on a low comfortable note and glide slowly up as high as is easy, then slide back down. Keep the sound light and quiet. If your voice flips, that is okay: go slower and softer. Do four glides and note where the sound changes.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('registers','mid-voice-vowels','Middle-voice vowel phrases','Choose five middle-range notes in a comfortable pattern (for example 1-2-3-2-1). Sing them on ''neh'' or ''nah'' with a light, connected sound. Keep the volume medium-low and the jaw easy. Move the pattern up and down by one note each time. Record and listen for a smooth, even sound.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('registers','light-landing','Light on the way up, soft on the way down','Sing a simple rising phrase from a song. On the way up, lighten your sound instead of pushing. On the way down, keep the same softness. Record and listen for any sudden louder bump on the high notes.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('registers','register-in-song','Move through registers in a song','Choose a song with a wide range. Mark where the melody moves to your higher area. Practice that move slowly, then in rhythm. Try it two ways: a smoother, connected sound and a fuller sound. Record both and choose the one that fits the song.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('agility','staccato-five','Staccato five-note pattern','Sing a five-note scale up and down on short, light ''ha ha ha ha ha'' notes, each separate, at a slow tempo. Keep your belly and jaw loose. When it feels clean, connect the notes smoothly on ''nah''. Record the staccato version and the connected version.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('agility','slow-run-groups','Slow run in small groups','Pick a short run from a song you love. Slow down the recording or hum it slowly. Break it into groups of three notes. Sing each group on ''noo'' until it is clean. Then join two groups. Keep it slow until you can sing it ten times in a row without mistakes.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('agility','nine-note-tempo','Nine-note pattern with a metronome','Sing a nine-note pattern (1-2-3-4-5-4-3-2-1) on ''yah'' or ''noo'' with a metronome at a slow tempo. After three clean rounds, increase the tempo by a small amount. Stop when notes become messy. Write your best tempo.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('agility','copy-and-vary','Copy a riff, then change one note','Choose a short riff you enjoy. Learn it slowly until it is clean. Then change just one note and sing it again. Record both versions and decide which you like.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('agility','riff-in-song','Place a run in a song','Pick a song. Choose one place at the end of a phrase for a short run. Practise it slowly, then with the track. Keep the run short and clear, and make sure the main melody still stands out. Record and listen: did the run serve the song?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('harmony','unison-blend','Unison blend','Play a track or sing along with a recording. Match the singer''s note, vowel shape and volume as closely as you can. Try to make your voice disappear inside theirs. Record and listen: can you hear two voices or one?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('harmony','hold-your-note','Hold your note','Play two notes together (or ask a helper to sing one). Sing the lower one steadily while the other note sounds. Don''t move toward the other note. Hold for eight counts. Switch to the other note.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('harmony','thirds','Simple thirds','Choose a simple melody (like ''Happy Birthday''). Sing the melody while a helper (or a keyboard) plays a note a third higher or lower. Then try to sing the third while the melody plays. Go slowly and listen for the sweet sound of the interval.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('harmony','part-over-track','Sing a part over a recorded track','Use a recording of a simple song. Learn a harmony part (from your instructor or by ear). Sing it with the track at a softer volume than the lead. Record and listen: do the parts blend?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('harmony','group-blend','Blend in a small group','Sing a simple chorus with two or three others (or layer your own recorded parts). Listen to the whole sound. Adjust your volume, vowel, and timing to blend. Record and ask: who stands out? Adjust until no one does.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('stamina-dynamics','soft-steady','Soft and steady','Sing ''ah'' on one comfortable note very softly for eight counts. Keep it steady and clear, not whispery. Try it again slightly louder. Notice how much air you use. Record and listen for steadiness.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('stamina-dynamics','swell','Swell and fade','On one comfortable note, begin softly, grow gradually louder over four counts, and then fade back over four counts. Keep the tone clear and the throat relaxed. Don''t let the pitch change. Record and listen for smoothness.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('stamina-dynamics','song-set','Build a short set','Choose two songs you know. Warm up for three minutes. Sing both back to back at a comfortable volume, then rest for two minutes. Notice where your voice tires. Write it down. Next week, add 30 seconds more.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',15),
 ('stamina-dynamics','plan-dynamics','Plan the dynamics of a song','Print or write the lyrics of a song. Mark the soft, medium and strong parts based on the meaning of the words. Practise with these in mind and record. Listen: do the dynamics help tell the story?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('interpretation','say-it-first','Say it, then sing it','Read the lyrics of a song aloud as though you are telling a friend something important. Underline the most important word in each line. Now sing the song and let those words stand out gently. Record and listen: do I sound like I mean it?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('interpretation','map-the-story','Map the story','Draw a simple line over the lyrics: where does the song start quiet, where does it build, where does it settle? Write one word beside each section (for example ''longing'', ''trust'', ''joy''). Sing the song following your map. Record and compare to a version where you ignore it.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('interpretation','eyes-and-face','Face and eyes','Sing a verse in front of a mirror or phone camera. Notice what your face does. Let it match the words in a natural way, without exaggeration. Record and watch once without sound: does your face tell the same story?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('interpretation','own-the-song','Make it yours','Choose a song you will share. Decide the tempo, the key and two places where you will do something different from the recording (such as a pause or a softer line). Sing it twice and record. Pick the version that feels most honest.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('performance-mic','mic-basics','Microphone basics','Hold a microphone (or a stand-in) about a hand-width from your mouth, pointing at your chin. Say a line quietly and loudly while keeping the same distance. Then sing a line and move the mic slightly away for louder notes and closer for softer ones. Record on your phone to hear the difference.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('performance-mic','pre-performance','Pre-performance routine','Before you sing for anyone, stand with relaxed shoulders, take two slow breaths, silently say why you are singing in one sentence, and then begin. Practise this routine before each practice recording this week.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',4),
 ('performance-mic','small-audience','Sing for one trusted person','Choose a trusted friend or family member. Sing one verse and chorus for them. Afterwards, ask: What did you hear? What was clear? Write down two things. Repeat next week with two listeners.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('performance-mic','recover','Recover from a mistake','Choose a song and sing it. When you make a mistake (or pretend to), don''t stop. Take a breath and rejoin the next line. Practise this three times. Record and notice how little the listener would have noticed.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',5),
 ('performance-mic','sound-check','Sound check routine','Practise a quick sound check: say a line, sing a few notes in your range, ask the sound person for one adjustment at a time (more of me, less of me, brighter, warmer). Practise the words politely and clearly.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('improvisation','echo-answer','Echo and answer','With a helper or a recording, listen to a two-bar phrase. First echo it back exactly. Then, next time, answer with something different but with a similar length. Take your time and keep the beat steady. Record your answers.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('improvisation','three-notes','Three notes, any order','Play a slow steady beat or a simple chord on a loop. Choose three notes (do, mi, sol). Sing any pattern you like using only those notes. Try different rhythms. Do not worry about sounding perfect. Record two minutes.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('improvisation','five-note-loop','Five-note scale over a loop','Use a simple chord loop (instructor-provided or your own). Use a five-note scale (1-2-3-5-6). Improvise a short phrase, rest for one bar, and answer your own phrase. Repeat for three minutes. Record and choose your favourite moment.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('improvisation','keep-a-favourite','Keep a favourite','Listen to your recording and find a phrase you like. Learn it so you can sing it again. Write how you would describe it. Next time, use it and change one thing.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('improvisation','shape-an-idea','Shape an idea','Improvise a 16-bar phrase with a clear beginning, middle and end. Decide where it will rise, where it will rest, and how it will finish. Record three attempts and note what changed.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('worship-leadership','song-prep-sheet','Song preparation sheet','For one song, write the key, tempo, song structure (verse, chorus, bridge), where you will breathe, and the first line you will sing. Note one sentence about what this song says. Practise leading it once using only your sheet.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('worship-leadership','clear-cues','Clear cues','Practise giving cues: a nod or hand signal to start, a clear lift into the chorus, a clear sign to end. Sing a verse and chorus alone and mark where each cue happens. Record on video and watch: would someone else understand?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('worship-leadership','two-song-transition','A two-song set with a transition','Choose two songs in related keys. Plan how you will move from one to the next: a held chord, a short spoken line, or a quiet pause. Practise the transition three times and record. Does it feel natural and unhurried?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('worship-leadership','adapting','When the plan changes','Practise a plan B: if the band drops out, if the key changes, if the lyrics screen fails. Sing a verse with one of these changes simulated and recover calmly. Write how you would communicate with the team.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('track-worship-leadership','one-sentence','One-sentence exhortation','Choose a song. Write one sentence you could say before the last chorus that points to what the song says (using Scripture if it fits your church practice). Say it aloud three times, then sing the chorus straight after. Record and listen: was it short, clear and unhurried?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('track-worship-leadership','flow-transition','Moving without interrupting','Plan three ways to move from one song to the next: (1) hold the last chord and begin the next song, (2) one spoken line over soft music, (3) a short pause. Practise each, recording yourself. Which kept the sense of worship unbroken?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-worship-leadership','repeat-and-tag','Lead a repeat or tag','Practise cueing a repeat of the chorus or a tag (repeating the last line). Agree a hand signal and a spoken backup. Sing with a backing track, choose one place to repeat, and give the cue early enough for others to follow. Record.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('track-worship-leadership','read-the-room','Read the room','After a set (or a practice with others), write what you noticed: did people sing, sit quietly, or look lost? What would you do differently? Plan one change for next time.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('track-spontaneous-expression','ad-lib-gaps','Ad-lib in the gaps (over a song)','Choose a song with a lead vocal. Listen and find the gaps between lines. Pick two gaps. Sing a very short phrase (two to four notes) in each, using notes from the key. Keep your volume below the lead singer. Record the song and listen: did your ad-lib support the lead?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',8),
 ('track-spontaneous-expression','call-response','Call and response','Listen to a short call phrase (from a track, a helper, or your instructor). Answer it with a related phrase of the same length. Repeat ten times, changing your answer each time. Record the best three.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('track-spontaneous-expression','instrumental-flow','Free-flow over instrumental-only music','Play an instrumental track with no vocal (provided by your instructor). Because no one else is singing, you can create full phrases. Start with two notes, add a third, and let a melody grow. Sing for two minutes without stopping. Record and listen for a phrase you liked.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-spontaneous-expression','compare-settings','Compare the two settings','Listen to your two recordings: an ad-lib over a song and a free-flow over instrumental. Write three differences in how you sang (length, volume, space). Write one thing you would change in each.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',6),
 ('track-spontaneous-expression','shaped-spontaneous','Shaped spontaneous passage','Over an instrumental, create a passage with a quiet beginning, a rising middle and a settled ending. Record three takes. Choose one and describe what shaped it.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-prophetic-worship','abide-time','Abiding time (private)','Choose a quiet time and place. Read John 15:4-5 slowly twice. Spend a few minutes in prayer, thanking God and asking him to speak in the way he chooses. Sit quietly for two minutes. Then sing or hum something simple in response if you wish. Afterwards write what you noticed. You do not need to label or force anything.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-prophetic-worship','scripture-to-song','Scripture to sung declaration','Choose a short verse (for example Colossians 3:16 or a psalm line). Say it aloud slowly. Find a simple melody of three or four notes. Sing the verse as a declaration, repeating it several times and letting the melody settle. Record a short take if you like, for your own review.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-prophetic-worship','pray-and-sing','Praying and singing with spirit and mind','Read 1 Corinthians 14:15. Spend five minutes in private worship. Alternate between praying in your own words and singing a simple line from Scripture. Write down one impression or phrase that came, and a verse that supports or tests it.

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-prophetic-worship','test-and-describe','Test and describe an impression','Choose one impression or phrase from your journal. Write: What did I sense? What Scripture speaks to it? What would be the effect if it were true (see 1 Corinthians 14:3 - does it build up, encourage or comfort)? How would I say it in one or two humble sentences?

Care: Stop and rest if you feel pain, tightness, burning, a catch in the voice, or light-headedness. Sing at a comfortable volume, drink water, and never push through strain. Ongoing hoarseness or discomfort lasting more than two weeks is a reason to see a doctor.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10),
 ('track-prophetic-worship','optional-tongues','Optional: singing in tongues','This is offered only if you have asked for it and it fits your own beliefs and church tradition. You can skip it without any consequence. If you choose to, spend a short time in private worship and sing in the way you are already accustomed to, in line with 1 Corinthians 14:15. Note how you felt and whether it fits your understanding of Scripture.

Care: Never assign or encourage this unless the student explicitly requested it and it matches their beliefs.

Record in 3–5 minute segments if you submit a recording.',10)
